      "description": "FLAGSHIP SIMULATION — no initial position is implied.\n\nContext: Recent UNESCO work distinguishes participation from genuine influence and provides global measurement plus institutional examples of youth recommendations entering formal processes.\n\nTrigger: A fictional public body is redesigning its youth participation mechanism after participants report that consultations rarely affect final decisions.\n\nDecision task: Design representation rules, agenda rights, feedback/accountability mechanisms, resourcing, accessibility safeguards and measures of actual influence.\n\nCommon evidence baseline: 2026 Youth Report: Lead with youth; 14th Edition of the UNESCO Youth Forum; UNESCO Youth Forum Process — Core Data Portal.\n\nStructured casting, evidence URLs, uncertainties, event injects, round plan and cross-World links are in the seed flagship source file.",
      "status": "open",
      "category": "youth"
    },
    {
      "key": "gender_equality_knowledge_systems",
      "title": "[DEMO] Which interventions best address persistent gender inequality in education and knowledge systems?",
      "description": "Examine social structures, education, policy, law and ethical trade-offs.",
      "status": "open",
      "category": "inclusion"
    },
    {
      "key": "bioethics_emerging_technology",
      "title": "[DEMO] How should emerging technologies be governed when potential benefit and ethical uncertainty are both high?",
      "description": "FLAGSHIP SIMULATION — no initial position is implied.\n\nContext: UNESCO adopted a Recommendation on the Ethics of Neurotechnology in 2025. Its work highlights potential benefit alongside concerns involving dignity, autonomy, mental privacy, unequal access and interaction with AI.\n\nTrigger: A fictional regulator is asked to set conditions for a consumer neurotechnology that infers cognitive or affective states outside a clinical setting.\n\nDecision task: Define permitted uses, consent requirements, mental-privacy safeguards, age/vulnerability protections, oversight and redress.\n\nCommon evidence baseline: Recommendation on the Ethics of Neurotechnology; Ethics of neurotechnology; Recommendation on the Ethics of Artificial Intelligence; Social and Human Sciences Sector at a glance.\n\nStructured casting, evidence URLs, uncertainties, event injects, round plan and cross-World links are in the seed flagship source file.",
      "status": "open",
      "category": "bioethics"
    }
  ],
